prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a 3D block tower projectile collapse simulation scene.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:

<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Scene Layout]
(1) Ground: a 30x30 gray plane at y=0;
(2) Block tower: 10 levels, with 3 blocks per level. Odd-numbered levels (levels 1/3/5...) are arranged parallel to the X-axis, while even-numbered levels are arranged parallel to the Z-axis (similar to Jenga-style cross-stacking). Each block has dimensions 3x1x1, and the material color alternates by level (such as ochre/brown). The center of the bottom of the block tower is at (0, 0, 0);

[Two-Stage Physics Model]
The key design of this scene: the block tower has two states—
- Static mode (kinematic): blocks remain fixed and are not affected by physics. Initially, all blocks are static.
- Dynamic mode (dynamic): blocks are affected by gravity and collisions, and can move and collapse.

State transition rules:
(3) Initially, all 30 blocks are in static mode—they remain stably fixed as though glued together;
(4) When a sphere hits a static block, that block and all blocks in the levels above it (with higher y coordinates) switch to dynamic mode and begin to be affected by physics;
(5) Dynamic blocks are affected by gravity g=9.8 m/s^2 and bounce when they collide with the ground or other blocks (restitution=0.3, friction=0.5);
(6) Collision detection: use AABB detection for sphere-block, block-block (only between dynamic blocks), and block-ground collisions. Treat sphere-block detection as bounding sphere vs AABB;
(7) Collision response: after detecting a collision, separate the two objects along the axis of minimum penetration and rebound the velocity components according to restitution. Sphere mass=2.0 and block mass=1.0; during collisions, distribute velocity changes according to the mass ratio (conservation of momentum);

[Projectile Ball Interaction]
(8) Click the scene to launch a red sphere from the camera position in the clicked direction (radius 0.5, initial speed 15 m/s). The sphere is affected by gravity and collides with blocks and the ground;
(9) At most 10 balls may exist simultaneously. When there are more than 10 balls, remove the earliest launched ball;

[Additional Interaction]
(10) Reset button (id="resetBtn", upper-right corner): when clicked, remove all balls and restore all blocks to their initial positions and static mode;
(11) Scoreboard: knocked-down blocks (dynamic blocks with y < 0.3) count as fallen blocks;

[HUD and State Exposure]
(12) The HUD in the upper-left corner displays:
  - Number of static blocks (id="staticCount")
  - Number of dynamic blocks (id="dynamicCount")
  - Number of fallen blocks (id="fallenCount")
  - Number of launched balls (id="ballCount")
(13) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame with:
  - blocks: an array in which each item contains { x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, isKinematic: boolean }
  - balls: an array in which each item contains { x, y, z, vx, vy, vz }
  - staticBlockCount: number of static blocks (those with isKinematic=true)
  - dynamicBlockCount: number of dynamic blocks (those with isKinematic=false)
  - fallenBlockCount: number of fallen blocks (those with isKinematic=false and y < 0.3)
  - ballCount: number of balls
  - towerIntact: boolean indicating whether all blocks are still static (no dynamic blocks)

[OrbitControls]
(14) Support mouse-drag view rotation. The camera is initially positioned at (15, 12, 15) and looks toward the origin.

Output a single HTML file that can run when opened directly in a browser.
